WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.922
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:13.700
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yep, I
am catching some Pokemons.

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:16.110
Yeah, you know,
"Pokemon Go" is crazy.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:20.430
Millions of people play this
game, and it scales like that.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:23.820
Did you know that it was
built on Google Cloud?

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:27.330
Well, let's talk to technical
manager at Niantic Labs

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:31.680
to take a behind the scenes look
at "Pokemon Go"'s architecture,

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:34.920
and how it scales for a
large number of players.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.405
Hi, James, welcome to the show.

00:00:37.405 --> 00:00:39.280
JAMES PROMPANYA: Hi,
thank you for having me.

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:40.590
I'm excited to be here.

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:42.007
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Well, for those

00:00:42.007 --> 00:00:43.980
who may not be familiar
with "Pokemon Go,"

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:45.970
let's quickly go
over what it is.

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:47.790
JAMES PROMPANYA: So
"Pokemon Go" is not

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:49.330
your typical mobile game.

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:52.530
It's a game that involves
walking around, catching

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:55.740
these little creatures around
you called Pokemon that are

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:58.200
appearing in the real world.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.710
It encourages you to go
outside, explore, and discover

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:03.510
things using augmented reality.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:05.933
A big part of that
which wasn't included

00:01:05.933 --> 00:01:07.350
in the game when
it first came out

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:11.550
was this big community
aspect of it.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:16.230
Players would go out and meet
with other players in real life

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:18.900
to play together,
battle in gyms,

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.490
and even like point
it out when there

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:23.850
was this rare, powerful Pokemon
that would appear somewhere

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.790
in your map.

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:28.470
Everyone sees the same Pokemon.

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:32.100
So you even see this in like
YouTube clips out there,

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.920
but sometimes somebody
would just yell out,

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:36.720
hey, there's a
Charizard here, and you

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:40.960
would see crowds of Pokemon
running towards that direction.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:44.070
So nowadays we really like
this part of the game.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:49.290
So we made it a major part by
hosting regular live events,

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:53.160
such as community days,
raid hours, Team Go Rocket

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:54.090
takeovers.

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:57.660
It all culminates in
this big summer event

00:01:57.660 --> 00:01:58.830
that we call Go Fest.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:00.997
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So it's
clear that lots of people

00:02:00.997 --> 00:02:03.870
are playing at any given time,
especially during Go Fest.

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:06.210
How does "Pokemon
Go" back end actually

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:08.639
scale to handle these
peaks in traffic?

00:02:08.639 --> 00:02:11.039
JAMES PROMPANYA: So
we use Google Cloud.

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:13.800
We have lots of services
that need scaling,

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.670
such as the Google Kubernetes
Engine and Spanner.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:20.220
Our front end service
is hosted on GKE.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.620
And it's pretty easy to
scale the notes there.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:24.870
Google Cloud provides
us with all the tools

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:27.480
we need to manage the
Kubernetes cluster.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.720
The Cloud Console is
just really easy to use

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:34.050
and comes with detailed
monitoring, graphs, tools,

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:35.490
logging all included.

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:37.050
And it's just a few clicks away.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:40.770
In the past Go Fest we
had Google engineers

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:43.530
virtually sitting
with us side by side

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:48.360
and ready to tackle any issues
that could come from the event.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:51.030
It was like having an extra
support team working directly

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:52.890
for us.

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:58.410
To give an idea of scale, we
have about 5,000 spanner nodes

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:01.260
handling the traffic, and
thousands of Kubernetes

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:04.110
nodes running just for
the "Pokemon Go" service.

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:07.710
We also have the
various microservices

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:13.170
that host other parts of the
game that help augment the game

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:14.370
experience.

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:16.620
All of these work together
to support millions

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:20.640
of players playing the game all
across the world at any given

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:21.360
moment.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.280
And if you compare
ourselves to like,

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:24.810
maybe "World of
Warcraft," you'll

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:28.410
see that other massively
multiplayer online games,

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:31.350
they split players
into multiple realms.

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:35.340
But for us, all of our players,
they reside on a single realm.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:38.970
It was important for us
that the players can always

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:42.270
interact with each other
and share the same game

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:45.630
experience no matter where
or when they're playing.

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:47.460
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Yeah, it's like making

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:50.670
sure it's one same world,
and one same similar

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:53.910
view that you're sharing with
all the players, which is

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:56.940
what makes the game so amazing.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:59.520
You mentioned Spanner and GKE.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:01.860
Was it always Spanner and GKE?

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:05.100
Or did you decide to make
that architectural decision

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:06.570
as the game got popular?

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:08.362
JAMES PROMPANYA: So we
actually started off

00:04:08.362 --> 00:04:09.480
with Google Data Store.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:11.550
It was an easy
way for us to just

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:13.830
get started without
having to worry

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:15.870
about another piece
of infrastructure

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:17.519
that we needed to
manage, and scale,

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:19.930
and deal with all those things.

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:22.830
However, as the game
matured, we decided

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:26.610
that we needed more control
over the size, and the scale,

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:28.500
and performance of the database.

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:32.880
So when we were looking
at different providers,

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:38.760
we saw that Spanner has
consistent indexing that

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:40.890
allows us to do more
complex database

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:44.350
schemas with these primary
and secondary keys.

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:46.740
Data Store was
also non-relational

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:49.750
with atomic and
durable transactions.

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:55.080
So we needed a relational
database with full consistency,

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:56.790
and made the choice
to go to Spanner,

00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:59.222
which gave us these
global asset transactions.

00:04:59.222 --> 00:05:01.680
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Well, it's
great to see that you started

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:05.040
off with a single back end
that worked at the time,

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:08.590
and then quickly evolved
that architecture to include

00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:12.760
the components that made
more sense as the game grew.

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:14.680
Now, let's say I am a player.

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:16.870
And I'm playing
the game right now.

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:19.570
I open my app to
catch a Pokemon.

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:21.610
What is happening
behind the scenes?

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:23.318
How does the request flow work?

00:05:23.318 --> 00:05:25.360
JAMES PROMPANYA: When the
user catches a Pokemon,

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:29.380
we receive that request through
the Google Cloud load balancer.

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:33.370
All of the static media is also
stored on Google Cloud Storage

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:37.240
buckets and is downloaded
to the phone when you first

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:38.500
start the app.

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:40.960
We also have caching enabled
at the load balancer.

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:46.360
So it's all cached and served
through Google Cloud SDN.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:49.870
So when the player
catches the Pokemon,

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:54.370
the GCLB sends a request
to our GKE cluster.

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:56.800
And we have a front
end service that sits

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:59.980
behind a Nginx reverse proxy.

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:02.710
The request goes from the user's
phone to the reverse proxy,

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:06.730
to one of these player
front end services.

00:06:06.730 --> 00:06:10.270
We also have this thing called
a spacial query back end.

00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:12.560
It's a cache by location.

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:18.130
So that is where we store the
information that determines

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:20.620
where a Pokemon is
shown on the map, what

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:23.740
gyms and Pokestops are around
you, what time zone you're in,

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:27.550
or any other feature
that is location-based.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:30.160
The way I like to think
about it is the front end

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:33.400
manages the player and their
interaction with the game,

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:37.360
while the spatial query
back end handles the map.

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:39.310
The front end retrieves
the information

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:42.260
from the [? squibby ?]
to send back to the user.

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:44.290
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So
the spatial back end,

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:47.860
it sounds like it's a custom
database of sorts that presents

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:49.780
the map and the Pokemons.

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:54.660
Now, what happens when I hunt
a Pokemon down and catch it?

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:56.410
JAMES PROMPANYA: When
you catch a Pokemon,

00:06:56.410 --> 00:06:59.530
it sends the request to the
front end to the Spanner

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:02.740
Database where your player
entity is stored in the store

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:04.060
there.

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:05.860
For catching Pokemon,
that actually

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:08.920
doesn't go to the
spatial query back end.

00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:12.550
If you were instead
battling in a gym,

00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:15.310
or adding lures to the
Pokestop, that information

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:18.070
is stored on the
spatial query back end.

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:21.250
And then the data gets
eventually consistent,

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:26.110
at which point the players
will receive the update.

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:29.890
And then it's used for
all the other players

00:07:29.890 --> 00:07:31.780
that are in your vicinity.

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:34.570
The front end at that point
will retrieve the information

00:07:34.570 --> 00:07:39.530
from the spatial query back end
and send it back to the user.

00:07:39.530 --> 00:07:42.280
We also store this
information using Protobuf

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:46.390
and additionally, we
also write this data

00:07:46.390 --> 00:07:50.890
into Bigtable, which we also
use for logging and tracking

00:07:50.890 --> 00:07:55.310
purposes with very strict
retention policies.

00:07:55.310 --> 00:07:59.410
We also use Pub/Sub to
send kind of a message

00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:01.810
to a Pub/Sub topic,
which is then

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:04.958
used for an analytics pipeline.

00:08:04.958 --> 00:08:07.000
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So how
do you ensure that when

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:09.700
two people who are in the
same geographic regions

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:14.050
see the same Pokemon data, and
keep that relatively in sync,

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:15.730
especially during the events?

00:08:15.730 --> 00:08:17.938
JAMES PROMPANYA: So it's
actually pretty interesting.

00:08:17.938 --> 00:08:20.380
Everything in our servers
are deterministic.

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:24.430
So even if players are
on different machines,

00:08:24.430 --> 00:08:29.650
they are sent to different
front end services,

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:31.390
if they're in the same
physical location,

00:08:31.390 --> 00:08:33.730
all the inputs
would be the same.

00:08:33.730 --> 00:08:36.880
And the same Pokemon would
get returned to both users.

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:38.620
There's a lot of
caching involved,

00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:42.820
and as well as precise timing,
especially when the settings

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:45.800
are changed and they need to be
in sync across all the servers.

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:49.600
So it's very tricky, but it
was very important for us

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:51.760
that all the players
feel like they're

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:53.592
part of this one shared world.

00:08:53.592 --> 00:08:55.300
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Speaking of analytics,

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:56.842
we talked a little
bit about Pub/Sub.

00:08:56.842 --> 00:08:58.420
And you're sending
these messages

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:00.590
into Pub/Sub for analytics.

00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:02.920
Now, there's a massive
amount of data that must

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:04.910
be generated during the game.

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:07.150
How does data analytics
pipeline work?

00:09:07.150 --> 00:09:09.485
And what are you analyzing?

00:09:09.485 --> 00:09:11.110
JAMES PROMPANYA:
Yeah, you are correct.

00:09:11.110 --> 00:09:13.330
There's like 10 to
20 GB of data that

00:09:13.330 --> 00:09:17.080
gets generated on a daily
basis, and stores all of it

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:18.980
into BigQuery.

00:09:18.980 --> 00:09:23.860
This game data is of interest
to our data science team.

00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:29.770
They use this for marketing
purposes, for verification.

00:09:29.770 --> 00:09:34.930
For example, we might want to
say that this player or players

00:09:34.930 --> 00:09:38.770
caught X millions
amount of Pokemon

00:09:38.770 --> 00:09:41.230
during our Saturday
community day events.

00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:43.960
So it's important that
we have that information

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:46.640
in an easily accessible manner.

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:49.810
We also use Dataflow as
our data processing engine

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:52.810
to run batch jobs to
process the player logs

00:09:52.810 --> 00:09:54.550
that are sitting in Bigtable.

00:09:54.550 --> 00:09:57.790
One example of that is
using cheat detection.

00:09:57.790 --> 00:10:00.010
We might look through
logs and notice

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:01.930
that a player used
to be in Japan,

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:04.660
and then a minute later
they were in Australia.

00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:07.540
So we want to check
for that, and make sure

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:12.820
that we're responding to
improper player signals.

00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:15.340
On the other side
of things, we also

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:19.660
use Google Dataflow to set
up Pokestops, and gyms,

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:22.360
and habitat information
all over the world.

00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:25.570
We take in information
from various sources,

00:10:25.570 --> 00:10:29.020
like OpenStreetMap, the
US Geological Survey,

00:10:29.020 --> 00:10:32.650
and Wayfarer, which is
our crowdsourced website

00:10:32.650 --> 00:10:36.880
for players to submit
points of interests

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:40.270
in their neighborhood, and
combine all that information

00:10:40.270 --> 00:10:42.048
to build a living
map of the world.

00:10:42.048 --> 00:10:43.840
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Now,
as the events grow,

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:46.030
like you mentioned, in
some of these events

00:10:46.030 --> 00:10:49.300
the traffic grows to
millions of users per second.

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:53.140
You also said 10 to 20
GB of data is generated.

00:10:53.140 --> 00:10:56.760
How does the system scale when
it comes to data pipeline?

00:10:56.760 --> 00:10:59.260
JAMES PROMPANYA: Yeah, so with
this increase of transactions

00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:01.690
there is increase of load
throughout the system

00:11:01.690 --> 00:11:04.060
with a lot of our
data pipelines.

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:07.990
For things like BigQuery,
a lot of that just works.

00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:11.320
Google Cloud will handle
the increase in traffic

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:13.312
without any intervention.

00:11:13.312 --> 00:11:14.770
Some of the other
things we'll just

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:17.410
scale preemptively
and make sure we

00:11:17.410 --> 00:11:20.950
work with Google to
make sure that we

00:11:20.950 --> 00:11:22.150
can support that traffic.

00:11:22.150 --> 00:11:23.900
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
With that much traffic,

00:11:23.900 --> 00:11:26.560
obviously the health of the
system is critical as well.

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:29.470
How do you monitor the
health of the system

00:11:29.470 --> 00:11:31.360
during these massive events?

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:34.490
JAMES PROMPANYA: So we use
multiple monitoring systems,

00:11:34.490 --> 00:11:37.637
including Prometheus, Grafana,
and Google Cloud Monitoring.

00:11:37.637 --> 00:11:39.220
On a personal level,
I actually prefer

00:11:39.220 --> 00:11:41.110
to Google Cloud
Monitoring, because it's

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:42.700
very simple to use.

00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:45.220
For me, it's very
easy to set up graphs,

00:11:45.220 --> 00:11:49.600
to set up alerts, and
not have to get really

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:53.350
into the nitty gritty
details of some

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:54.730
of these other implementations.

00:11:54.730 --> 00:11:57.760
Everything just is very easy to
use in Google Cloud and Google

00:11:57.760 --> 00:11:58.780
Cloud Monitoring.

00:11:58.780 --> 00:12:00.738
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Well,
it was awesome to hear

00:12:00.738 --> 00:12:03.850
the entire implementation
and the architecture details.

00:12:03.850 --> 00:12:06.820
I am very sure you
have something in mind,

00:12:06.820 --> 00:12:10.180
or you're cooking up something
to evolve this architecture

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:11.410
right now.

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:14.650
What are some of the expansions
and improvements you're

00:12:14.650 --> 00:12:16.143
looking to do in the future?

00:12:16.143 --> 00:12:18.310
JAMES PROMPANYA: On the
Google Cloud side of things,

00:12:18.310 --> 00:12:22.450
we are looking into things
such as Agones and Google Game

00:12:22.450 --> 00:12:26.110
Servers, and using
that to see how we

00:12:26.110 --> 00:12:27.910
can make our game even better.

00:12:27.910 --> 00:12:30.310
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: We wish
you and the Niantic team best

00:12:30.310 --> 00:12:32.440
of luck for these enhancements.

00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:36.460
And this has obviously been
super insightful for us, James.

00:12:36.460 --> 00:12:39.370
Thanks for joining me today
and sharing your architecture

00:12:39.370 --> 00:12:40.340
with us.

00:12:40.340 --> 00:12:41.270
JAMES PROMPANYA: Yeah,
thanks for having me.

00:12:41.270 --> 00:12:41.833
This was fun.

00:12:41.833 --> 00:12:43.750
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Well,
we just took a behind

00:12:43.750 --> 00:12:46.090
